# Git applies these rules top to bottom, and a later rule overrides an
# earlier one for the same attribute. The general rule therefore comes
# first and the binary rules come last, so nothing can override them.

# Text files use LF in the repository and native endings on checkout.
* text=auto

# Raw data must stay byte-identical on every checkout, because the run
# manifest verifies its SHA-256 hash. Git never converts these files.
data/raw/** binary
# Generated data files are hashed in the manifest too.
data/processed/** binary
# Human label and provenance files use stable byte handling for their checks.
# Spreadsheets are binary; CSV keeps its exact bytes (no line-ending
# conversion) but still diffs as text; the TOML records always use LF.
data/labels/*.xlsx binary
data/labels/*.csv -text
data/labels/*.toml text eol=lf
# The arrangement labelling guide is hashed in its pilot record (D-041).
docs/arrangement_labelling_guide.md text eol=lf
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.pptx binary
*.sqlite binary
